  "notes": [
    "Nachemson 1964/1970 and Schultz 1982 are early-transducer values from the Dreischarf 2016 review (Nachemson corrected by -0.25 MPa): shown, not pooled.",
    "Wilke 2001 is one 72 kg male; his lifts are dynamic peaks, his flexion angles reconstructed from the lumbar record.",
    "Lying IDP is mostly disc swelling pressure, which compression-driven models underpredict."
  ],
  "agent_prompt": "Use your spine musculoskeletal model. For each task in \"tasks\", simulate the posture, trunk angle and hand load given in its inputs for the subject of each study with a ref in that task (see \"subjects\": sex, age, height, body mass, disc area; for studies with individual refs the cohort mean subject is enough). Compute compression and flexion at L4-L5 (L3-L4 for standing_l3l4) and convert to intradiscal pressure with Ghezelbash 2016 Eq. 6 (P = F/A) or IDP = F/(0.66 A); say which. Do not tune the model to the reference values. Return JSON shaped like \"answer_format\": one entry per task id with IDP in MPa, or an object {study_key: number} when you simulated several subjects for that task.",
